Sophistry is argument used to win, impress, obscure, or manipulate rather than genuinely discover what is true.

A sophist may use valid-sounding logic, selective framing, ambiguity, emotional leverage, or rhetorical tricks while quietly protecting a conclusion they already want.

The useful contrast is:

reasoning: “What conclusion does the evidence support?”
sophistry: “How can I make my conclusion seem supported?”

It is not the same as simply being wrong. Someone can reason sincerely and still be mistaken. Sophistry involves a distortion in the relationship between argument and truth-seeking.

Related terms include casuistry, specious reasoning, rhetoric, equivocation, rationalization, motivated reasoning, dialectical trickery, and eristic argument—argument aimed primarily at victory.